{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"t1_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **0:00 - 1:00**\n\n**Connect from Part 3**","x":0,"y":0,"width":300,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t1_notes","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Key notes\n>\n> In Part 3, we learned how data is stored. Now we learn how tasks are organized using functions. A function is a reusable block of code.","x":-400,"y":0,"width":350,"height":150,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"t2_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **1:00 - 5:00**\n\n**What is a function & components**","x":0,"y":250,"width":300,"height":150,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t2_notes","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Key notes\n>\n> A function is a named block of code that performs a specific task.","x":-400,"y":250,"width":350,"height":140,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"t2_board","type":"text","text":"> [!example] Board / slide text\n>\n> `int` -> return type\n> `add` -> function name\n> `int a, int b` -> parameters\n> `{ return a + b; }` -> function body\n> `add(10, 20)` -> function call","x":350,"y":250,"width":350,"height":220,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t2_pyq","type":"text","text":"> [!abstract] PYQ-ready answer\n>\n> The main components of a function are return type, function name, parameter list, function body, function call and function declaration/prototype if used.","x":350,"y":500,"width":350,"height":200,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t3_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **5:00 - 8:00**\n\n**Declaration, definition & call**","x":0,"y":750,"width":300,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t3_notes","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Key notes\n>\n> Declaration tells compiler that the function exists. Definition tells what the function actually does. Call means use the function.","x":-400,"y":750,"width":350,"height":150,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"t3_board","type":"text","text":"> [!example] Board / slide text\n>\n> **Declaration** -> promise\n> **Definition** -> actual work\n> **Call** -> execution","x":350,"y":750,"width":350,"height":150,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t3_mistake","type":"text","text":"> [!warning] Common mistake\n>\n> A declaration without a definition can lead to a linking error if the function is called.","x":-400,"y":950,"width":350,"height":120,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"t4_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **8:00 - 13:00**\n\n**Call by value**","x":0,"y":1200,"width":300,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t4_notes","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Key notes\n>\n> Call by value means a copy of the actual argument is passed to the function. Changes made inside the function do not affect the original variable.","x":-400,"y":1200,"width":350,"height":240,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"t4_board","type":"text","text":"> [!example] Board / slide text\n>\n> **Output idea:**\n> Inside function x = 100\n> Inside main a = 10","x":350,"y":1200,"width":350,"height":150,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t4_code","type":"text","text":"**Code to teach**\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\nvoid change(int x) {\n x = 100;\n cout << \"Inside function x = \" << x << endl;\n}\nint main() {\n int a = 10;\n change(a);\n cout << \"Inside main a = \" << a << endl;\n return 0;\n}\n```","x":750,"y":1200,"width":350,"height":420,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"t4_pyq","type":"text","text":"> [!abstract] PYQ-ready answer\n>\n> Call by value means a copy of the actual argument is passed to the function. Changes made inside the function affect only the copy, not the original variable.","x":350,"y":1400,"width":350,"height":180,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t5_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **13:00 - 18:00**\n\n**Call by reference**","x":0,"y":1750,"width":300,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t5_notes","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Key notes\n>\n> Call by reference means the function receives the reference of the original variable. Changes made inside the function affect the original variable.","x":-400,"y":1750,"width":350,"height":150,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"t5_board","type":"text","text":"> [!example] Board / slide text\n>\n> **Output idea:**\n> Inside function x = 100\n> Inside main a = 100","x":350,"y":1750,"width":350,"height":150,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t5_pyq","type":"text","text":"> [!abstract] PYQ-ready answer\n>\n> Call by reference means the function receives a reference/address of the original variable. Changes made inside the function directly affect the original variable. In C++, reference is written using `&`: `void change(int &x)`.","x":350,"y":1950,"width":350,"height":270,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t6_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **18:00 - 22:00**\n\n**Recursive function**","x":0,"y":2350,"width":300,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t7_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **22:00 - 27:00**\n\n**Function overloading**","x":0,"y":3000,"width":300,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t7_board","type":"text","text":"> [!example] Board / slide text\n>\n> Overloading can be done by changing:\n> 1. Number of parameters\n> 2. Type of parameters\n> 3. Order of parameters\n>\n> Return type alone cannot overload a function.","x":350,"y":3000,"width":350,"height":200,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t7_code","type":"text","text":"**Code to teach**\n\n```cpp\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\n\ndouble add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }\n\nint add(int a, int b, int c) { return a + b + c; }\n\nint main() {\n cout << add(2, 3) << endl;\n cout << add(2.5, 3.5) << endl;\n cout << add(1, 2, 3) << endl;\n return 0;\n}\n```","x":750,"y":3000,"width":350,"height":440,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"t7_mistake","type":"text","text":"> [!warning] Common mistake\n>\n> `int show(int x);` and `double show(int x);` is invalid overloading because only the return type is different.","x":350,"y":3250,"width":350,"height":190,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"t8_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **27:00 - 29:00**\n\n**Inline function**","x":0,"y":3550,"width":300,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t8_board","type":"text","text":"> [!example] Board / slide text\n>\n> Instead of `square(5)`, compiler may treat it like `5 * 5`.\n> `inline` is a request to compiler, not a guarantee. Large or recursive functions may not become inline.","x":350,"y":3550,"width":350,"height":220,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t8_code","type":"text","text":"**Code to teach**\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\ninline int square(int x) {\n return x * x;\n}\nint main() {\n cout << square(5);\n return 0;\n}\n```","x":750,"y":3550,"width":350,"height":290,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"t9_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **29:00 - 30:00**\n\n**Generic function**","x":0,"y":3900,"width":300,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t9_code","type":"text","text":"**Code to teach**\n\n```cpp\ntemplate <class T>\nT maximum(T a, T b) {\n return (a > b) ? a : b;\n}\n```","x":350,"y":3900,"width":350,"height":180,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"t9_pyq","type":"text","text":"> [!abstract] PYQ-ready answer\n>\n> A generic function is written to work with different data types. In C++, it is commonly implemented using function templates. For a 2-mark answer, remember: one function logic can work with multiple data types.","x":750,"y":3900,"width":350,"height":240,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t10_time","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] **Final revision for Part 4**","x":0,"y":4200,"width":400,"height":120,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"t10_board","type":"text","text":"- **Function** = named block of code. Components = return type, name, parameters, body, call.\n- **Declaration** tells function exists. **Definition** contains actual logic. **Call** executes the function.\n- **Call by value** = copy passed, original unchanged.\n- **Call by reference** = reference passed, original can change.\n- **Recursive function** = function calls itself. Recursion must have base condition.\n- **Function overloading** = same name, different parameters. Overloading = compile-time polymorphism. Return type alone cannot overload.\n- **Inline** may replace function call with body.","x":0,"y":4350,"width":600,"height":300,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t4_motion","type":"text","text":"> [!tip] Motion graphics idea\n>\n> Show `a = 10` in main. The function receives a separate copy `x = 10`. Changing `x` does not change `a`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[main: a = 10] -- copy passed --> X[change: x = 10]\n  X -. changes to .-> X100[x = 100]\n  A -. remains .-> A10[a = 10]\n```","x":-1400,"y":1275,"width":800,"height":365,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t5_motion","type":"text","text":"> [!tip] Motion graphics idea\n>\n> Show `a` and `x` as two names for the same memory box. Changing `x` changes `a`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  Box[Memory Box: 10] --- NameA(Name: a)\n  Box --- NameX(Name: x)\n  Box -. change via x .-> Box100[Memory Box: 100]\n```","x":-1120,"y":1870,"width":520,"height":390,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t6_notes","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Key notes\n>\n> A recursive function is a function that calls itself. It must have a base condition to stop recursion.","x":-440,"y":2275,"width":350,"height":185,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"t7_motion","type":"text","text":"> [!tip] Motion graphics idea\n>\n> Show compiler choosing the correct `add()` function based on the arguments.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Call1[add 2, 3] -->|matches| F1[add int, int]\n  Call2[add 2.5, 3.5] -->|matches| F2[add double, double]\n```","x":-1080,"y":3460,"width":550,"height":400,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t8_notes","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Key notes\n>\n> An inline function is a function whose call may be replaced by the actual function code by the compiler. It reduces function call overhead for small functions.","x":-440,"y":3550,"width":350,"height":150,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"t9_notes","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Key notes\n>\n> Do not teach full templates deeply. Give a 1-minute safety note because generic function can appear as a short question.","x":-440,"y":4020,"width":350,"height":180,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"t8_pyq","type":"text","text":"> [!abstract] PYQ-ready answer\n>\n> An inline function is a function for which the compiler may replace the function call with the actual function body. It is useful for small frequently used functions because it can reduce function call overhead.","x":-440,"y":3770,"width":350,"height":220,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t6_code","type":"text","text":"**Code to teach**\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\nint factorial(int n) {\n if (n == 0 || n == 1) {\n return 1;\n }\n return n * factorial(n - 1);\n}\nint main() {\n int n = 5;\n cout << \"Factorial = \" << factorial(n);\n return 0;\n}\n```","x":1160,"y":2320,"width":350,"height":440,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"t7_pyq","type":"text","text":"> [!abstract] PYQ-ready answer\n>\n> Function overloading means defining two or more functions with the same name but different parameter lists in the same scope. The compiler decides which function to call based on number, type or order of arguments. It is an example of compile-time polymorphism.","x":1280,"y":3440,"width":350,"height":260,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t5_code","type":"text","text":"**Code to teach**\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\nvoid change(int &x) {\n x = 100;\n cout << \"Inside function x = \" << x << endl;\n}\nint main() {\n int a = 10;\n change(a);\n cout << \"Inside main a = \" << a << endl;\n return 0;\n}\n```","x":880,"y":1670,"width":350,"height":450,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"title","type":"text","text":"---\ncssclasses: cc-border-double, cc-card-nocolor\n---\n\n>[!cc-header] # Part 4: Functions\n\n<div style=\"height:30px\"></div>\n\n**Duration**: 30 Minutes\n\n**Scope**:\n1. What is a function?\n2. Function components\n3. Declaration, definition and call\n4. Call by value\n5. Call by reference\n6. Function vs recursive function\n7. Function overloading\n8. Compile-time polymorphism link\n9. Inline function\n10. Generic function/template idea","x":-200,"y":-600,"width":800,"height":510,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"t2_motion","type":"text","text":"> [!tip] Motion graphics idea\n>\n> Show function like a machine: input 10 and 20 -> add function -> output 30.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[Input: 10] --> F((add function))\n  B[Input: 20] --> F\n  F --> O[Output: 30]\n  style F fill:#f9f,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px\n```","x":-1040,"y":320,"width":520,"height":360,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t3_code","type":"text","text":"**Code to teach**\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n// Function declaration / prototype\nint add(int, int);\n\nint main() {\n int result = add(5, 7); // function call\n cout << \"Sum = \" << result;\n return 0;\n}\n\n// Function definition\nint add(int a, int b) {\n return a + b;\n}\n```","x":840,"y":660,"width":350,"height":500,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"t5_table","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Value vs Reference\n>\n| Basis | Call by Value | Call by Reference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Meaning** | Copy is passed | Reference/address is passed |\n| **Original value** | Not changed | Can be changed |\n| **Memory** | Extra copy is created | No extra copy |\n| **Syntax** | `void fun(int x)` | `void fun(int &x)` |\n| **Use** | When original should be safe | When original must be modified |","x":1335,"y":1690,"width":450,"height":460,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t6_pyq","type":"text","text":"> [!abstract] PYQ-ready answer\n>\n> A function is a named block of code that performs a task. A recursive function is a function that calls itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem. Every recursive function must have a base condition; otherwise infinite recursion occurs.","x":1160,"y":2820,"width":350,"height":280,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t2_code","type":"text","text":"**Code to teach**\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\nint add(int a, int b) {\n return a + b;\n}\nint main() {\n int result = add(10, 20);\n cout << \"Sum = \" << result;\n return 0;\n}\n```","x":800,"y":250,"width":350,"height":340,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"t1_board","type":"text","text":"> [!example] Board / slide text\n>\n> Data storage -> Arrays, strings, pointers\n> Task organization -> **Functions**","x":450,"y":0,"width":350,"height":180,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t7_notes","type":"text","text":"> [!info] Key notes\n>\n> This is the most important part of Part 4. Function overloading means defining multiple functions with the same name but different parameter lists.","x":-440,"y":3093,"width":350,"height":215,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"t6_motion","type":"text","text":"> [!tip] Motion graphics idea\n>\n> Animate a recursion stack. Each call waits for the smaller call to return. Then returns unwind upward.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  f5[factorial 5] -->|5 * f4| f4[factorial 4]\n  f4 -->|4 * f3| f3[factorial 3]\n  f3 -->|3 * f2| f2[factorial 2]\n  f2 -->|2 * f1| f1[factorial 1 = 1]\n  f1 -.->|1| f2\n  f2 -.->|2| f3\n  f3 -.->|6| f4\n  f4 -.->|24| f5\n```","x":-800,"y":2495,"width":300,"height":850,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"t6_board","type":"text","text":"> [!example] Board / slide text\n>\n> `factorial(5)`\n> `= 5 * factorial(4)`\n> `= 5 * 4 * factorial(3)`\n> `= 5 * 4 * 3 * factorial(2)`\n> `= 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * factorial(1)`\n> `= 120`","x":400,"y":2290,"width":350,"height":250,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"t6_mistake","type":"text","text":"> [!warning] Common mistake\n>\n> A recursive factorial without a base condition causes infinite recursion.","x":400,"y":2600,"width":350,"height":160,"color":"1"}
	],
